1 OCCU Stone mason,Magister 
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} The Bruttapelle family were stonemasons who built altars of marble and stone throughout Dalmatia. Their workshop was based in Vrboska, Hvar, Croatia. There were two lines in Croatia of Bruttapelles, one from Andrea
2 CONC  Bruttapelle (b.1728) and another through Antonio Brutapelle (b.1748), both of whom were born in Bassano del Grappa, Italy, and baptized at Santa Maria in Colle.  During this time, Bassano del Grappa and Vrboska were under the con
2 CONC trol of the Venetian Empire, from which funding for church projects in Dalmatia was abundant.
2 CONT
2 CONT Antonio Bruttapelle was born on 12 June 1748. By 1777, He had 7 siblings also born in Bassano. Magistri Antonio Bruttapelle had left his place of birth for Vrboska, where he appears to have joined Andrea and his family. That year,
2 CONC  Antonio presented as a Godfather to Jacoba Franichievich, daughter of Stephani, at Sv. Lovre in Vrboska.  By the time of this baptism, Antonio's father Jacobi/Giacomo had died. Antonio later served as a witness to the Starigrad, 
2 CONC Hvar, marriage of Magistri Giuseppe Costa. By 1791, Antonio had married Anastasia Coschich of Vrobska, and their son Giacomo was born that year. The couple would also have two daughters, Tomasina (b. 1795) and Maddalena (b. ~1797)
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT Antonio died in 1817 in Split, three years after his wife passed away in Vrboska. He was buried in Split at Sv. Stjepan. While his death record states he was 64 at the time of his death, he was actually 68.
2 CONT
2 CONT Note: Antonio's name was previously written on Geni.com as Ambrosius (Ambroz). This was due to a transcription error in a scholarly article written about Vrboska's population, which included Antonio's name written incorrectly. The
2 CONC  baptism document actually states the name as Antonius, not Ambrosius. The excerpt of the relevant 1777 baptism document is in the media section and is also available from the Croatian Archives in Zagreb. 
