1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * https://familysearch.org/pal:/MM9.3.1/TH-1971-28339-6975-49?cc=2040054&wc=MC5D-738:391644801,392240201,392246501
2 CONT * https://familysearch.org/pal:/MM9.3.1/TH-1942-28334-21506-65?cc=2040054&wc=9R2W-RM8:391644801,392196101,392196401
2 CONT
2 CONT * http://hbl.lzmk.hr/clanak.aspx?id=372
2 CONT
2 CONT * https://www.researchgate.net/publication/396263233_FADED_AND_REVIVED_-_FOLK_DRAMA_PASSION_PLAYS_AND_CUSTOMS_IN_THE_NERETVA_VALLEY
2 CONT
2 CONT * https://oraltradition.org/back-in-the-foundation/
2 CONT
2 CONT ALAČEVIĆ, Ante Franjin, sakupljač narodnih pjesama (Drvenik kod Makarske, 8. II 1781 — Makarska, 27. I 1856). Drugo je dijete Frane i Margarite, rođ. Batošić. Kao mlad javio se za kadeta u Linijsku regimentu »Medin« u Veneciji. Na
2 CONC  mletačkoj strani borio se 1797. protiv Francuza, bio zarobljen kod San Massima i do lipnja zatvoren u Milanu, a zatim je u vojsci na ušću rijeke Pad. Umirovljen je već 1800. kao pomorski časnik. Od 1803. bio je upravitelj Sanitar
2 CONC nog kordona Neretve i Makarske i više godina upravitelj pošte u Makarskoj. Ženio se dva puta: 1804. Jelenom Nonković, koja mu je rodila tri sina, a 1825. Marijom Anom Ivačić, s kojom je imao šest sinova i tri kćeri. Oslijepio je p
2 CONC et godina prije smrti. — A. je bio klasičan zapisivač narodne poezije po selima makarskog, imotskog; neretvanskog, vrgoračkog i omiškog kraja te obližnjih otoka. Neke pjesme kazivala mu je prva žena, a neke je čuo od seljaka Dalma
2 CONC tinske zagore. Njegov Pismar narodni sadrži 168 lirskih i epskih pjesama, od kojih su neke preuzete iz pisanih djela (npr. kanonika Stipana Borića). U zbirci ima i nekoliko Antinih pjesama; spominju se dvije spjevane u duhu A. Kač
2 CONC ića Miošića: Pisma Ivana bana iz Podgore, kako s Mozoovom zametnu rat Francuzu i Francuz pridobi i popali im kuće 1807. i Pisma kako Žažabov i Bilić na Makarsku dođoše godine 1809. Građu što su je sakupili Antini potomci i Pismar 
2 CONC narodni ustupio je unuk Miroslav 1888. MH (danas u Rukopisnom arhivu Odbora za narodni život i običaje JAZU). Dio pjesama ušao je i u Matičine zbirke Hrvatskih narodnih pjesama. — Pojedini su Antini zapisi poznati sve do danas, a 
2 CONC drži se da su preko njega dospjeli i u Vukovu zbirku (N. Andrić). Antinim zbornikom služili su se F. Miklošič, M. Pavlinović, I. Kukuljević Sakcinski i dr.
2 CONT
2 CONT LIT.: Ante Lulić: Compendio storico-cronologico di Macarsca e del suo Litorale, ossia Primorje. Split 1860, 93–103. — Frano Alačević: Memorie della famiglia Alačević. Dubrovnik 1884, 54–74. — Nikola Andrić: Otkud Vuku »Zidanje Ska
2 CONC dra«? Glas Matice hrvatske, 3(1908) 12, str. 97–100. — Isti: I opet Vukovo »Zidanje Skadra«. Ibid., 14/15, str. 116–121. — Isti: Još jedan mali rep Vukovu »Zidanju Skadra«. Ibid., str. 127–128. — Isti: Alačević i Karadžić. Ibid., 
2 CONC 16/17, str. 134–136; 18, str. 145–148. — Josip Pasarić: Andrićevo »iznenađenje«. Savremenik, 3(1908) 8, str. 487–491. — Jovan Skerlić: Otkud Vuku »Zidanje Skadra«? Srpski književni glasnik, 1908, XXI/1, str. 68–72. — Dušan Berić: 
2 CONC Priča o zakopanom blagu na otočiću Šćedru. Slobodna Dalmacija, 10(1952) br. 2453. — Gašpar Bujas: Kačićevi imitatori u Makarskom primorju do polovine 19. stoljeća. Građa za povijest književnosti hrvatske, 1971, 30, str. 145–159, 2
2 CONC 59–265.
2 CONT
2 CONT Josip Kekez (1983)
2 CONT
2 CONT Komentar 
2 CONT
2 CONT * Reference: [https://www.myheritage.com/research/record-1-70888891-1-262/ante-franjina-ten-antonio-graf-ala%C4%8Devi%C4%87-in-myheritage-family-trees?indId=externalindividual-e35bb3cdb845d7893ff2358dcacf4028&mrid=56f28c8038edaa9a
2 CONC bacebd45e5de89a1 MyHeritage Family Trees] - [https://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Apr 26 2020, 10:18:14 UTC''
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT =='''Venetian Military Service'''==
2 CONT
2 CONT He enlisted as a cadet in the Reggimento Medin, one of eleven Oltramarini regiments — the elite Dalmatian infantry corps of the Republic of Venice, organised within the Venetian navy. The regiment took its name from its colonel, c
2 CONC onsistent with documented Oltramarini naming practice. The Oltramarini were recruited primarily from the Slavic Catholic population of the Dalmatian coast, making Ante a typical recruit for this corps.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''The Veronese Easter, April 17–25, 1797:'''
2 CONT
2 CONT The Venetian Senate specifically dispatched the Medin Regiment to Verona to stabilise growing tensions with the French occupying forces. Instead, the regiment joined the civilian uprising against the French. This was the Veronese 
2 CONC Easter — the most significant anti-French rebellion in Italy during the entire Napoleonic period. The Medin Regiment, with approximately 550 Dalmatian soldiers, fought alongside roughly 600 Veronese citizens against a French garri
2 CONC son, initially defeating over a thousand French soldiers in the first hours of fighting. The uprising lasted nine days. On April 25, 1797, fifteen thousand French reinforcements encircled the city. Verona fell. French casualties t
2 CONC otalled approximately 500 dead, with around 1,000 wounded and 2,400 prisoners taken.
2 CONT Capture near San Massimo:
2 CONT
2 CONT The family record states Ante was captured near San Massimo. San Massimo is confirmed historically as a combat location on the western edge of Verona, consistent with the French encirclement of April 25. He was among the 2,400 pri
2 CONC soners taken when the city fell. His specific name does not appear in any surviving primary source document found — he is confirmed as a member of the documented regiment in the documented battle, but is not named individually in 
2 CONC any source recovered.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Imprisonment in Milan, to June 1797:'''
2 CONT
2 CONT He was imprisoned in Milan, the administrative capital of the French Cisalpine Republic, consistent with French practice for processing prisoners from the Veronese theater. His release in June 1797 is consistent with armistice arr
2 CONC angements following the Treaty of Leoben signed April 17, 1797. He was held for approximately two months.
2 CONT
2 CONT While Ante was imprisoned, on May 12, 1797, the thousand-year-old Republic of Venice formally dissolved. The Medin Regiment — the unit he served — was disbanded on the same date. He was released into a world where his state and hi
2 CONC s regiment no longer existed.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT =='''Transitional Service at the Po River Mouth, 1797–1800'''==
2 CONT
2 CONT The family record states he served in the army at the mouth of the Po River following his release, retiring as a naval officer in 1800. Historical sources confirm that former Oltramarini soldiers were operating as a task force und
2 CONC er Austrian command in exactly this region during 1799 — embarked on the former Venetian flotilla of Chioggia, operating between Comacchio and Mesola near the sea. By the Treaty of Campo Formio, October 1797, Dalmatia formally pas
2 CONC sed to Habsburg control, meaning any military service Ante performed from that point was effectively under Austrian direction, even if informally structured.
2 CONT
2 CONT His retirement designation as a "naval officer" in 1800 is consistent with the documented absorption of former Venetian naval personnel into Habsburg service — Dalmatian officers are confirmed to have accounted for 15 to 20 percen
2 CONC t of Habsburg sea officers following the Venetian transition. The exact rank he held is not confirmed in any source found.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT =='''Director of the Sanitary Cordon, Neretva and Makarska, from 1803'''==
2 CONT
2 CONT From 1803, Ante served as director of the Habsburg Sanitary Cordon covering the Neretva River and the Makarska coastline — the quarantine and border security system maintained along the Ottoman frontier. This was a formal Habsburg
2 CONC  appointment under the General Norm of Health Service, first codified in 1770.
2 CONT
2 CONT His responsibilities included commanding guard posts along the river and coast, running the lazaretto quarantine station, classifying all persons and goods crossing from Ottoman Bosnia-Herzegovina into Habsburg Dalmatia, enforcing
2 CONC  quarantine periods of up to 40 days, producing daily reports to superiors in Zadar, and coordinating with the provincial administration which reported ultimately to the Sanitary Court Commission in Vienna. His guards were authori
2 CONC sed to use lethal force against illegal crossers. Death penalties for quarantine violations were codified in imperial law.
2 CONT
2 CONT He sat in the middle tier of the Habsburg administrative hierarchy — below the provincial governor in Zadar, who reported to Vienna, and above the individual post commanders and lazaretto staff under his direct command. Quarantine
2 CONC  station personnel typically numbered up to 60 at a single station; across the full Neretva-Makarska section his total command likely ranged from 60 to 150 people in normal conditions.
2 CONT He was returning to the region of his origins — the Neretva and Makarska coastline was his home territory, and the Habsburg administration relied on officers with local knowledge, language, and standing to run sensitive border pos
2 CONC itions.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT =='''Postmaster, Makarska'''==
2 CONT
2 CONT For several years — the exact dates are not confirmed — Ante served as postmaster in Makarska. The Habsburg postal system was a crown monopoly and an imperial institution. The postmaster managed relay horses and riders, collected 
2 CONC postal revenue on behalf of the imperial treasury, handled official government dispatches, and maintained the communications network along the Dalmatian coast. The role carried formal state authority and a salary. Given Makarska's
2 CONC  position near the Ottoman frontier, the postmaster also handled correspondence crossing or adjacent to the border, a function with intelligence significance. The role ran alongside or closely followed his Sanitary Cordon director
2 CONC ship, and the same profile — military background, local knowledge, administrative competence, established Habsburg loyalty — suited both positions.
